Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillets: a rounding over the mouths of slots whose rounded ends touch the
top face along a crest line, built as a construction of its own when the
corners fail there (mitcad#1204).

The case: a rim of eight arcs (a circle of radius 93.5 between a top plane
and eight pieces of an outer cylinder, 2.17 high above a rounding of 5),
rounded with 2.5. At each of the eight vertices between the arcs a fin
1.5 thick stands out of the wall; its top is a cylinder of radius 0.75
about an axis 0.75 under the top, so that the top plane and that cylinder
touch along the crest line with no edge there. Four edges meet at each
vertex: the two arcs and the two curves where the fin's cylinder cuts the
two cylinder pieces. The chain stops there, and the corner's walk round
the vertex finds no edge between the top plane and the fin's cylinder
(ChFi3d_Builder_0.cxx, "Failed to find edge"): the fillet fails at all
eight vertices. The stored body after the rounding runs each arc's blend
on past the vertex over the fin's top, cut there by the fin's side wall
and then by its top cylinder, which grows inwards to the blends up to the
crest's point on the top plane, where the two blends meet.

BRepFilletAPI_MakeFillet::Build now tries BuildOverSlotMouths (a new
method, in the new BRepFilletAPI_MakeFillet_SlotMouths.cxx) where the
computation failed only at faulty vertices, each of them such a mouth
(FindSlotMouth): four edges there, two of them rounded with the same
constant radius, tangent there, between a plane T and two faces A and B;
the other two between A and a cylinder C and between B and C; C's radius
smaller than the rounding's, its axis parallel to T and under it by C's
radius, the vertex on the crest line. Its construction:
1. the body less a slab through each mouth, C's diameter wide, from four
   radii inside to four outside along C's axis, from three radii below the
   crest to one above (BRepAlgoAPI_Cut);
2. that body rounded with the same edges' images, radii and parameters
   (SetParams, StopChainsAtTurns): each arc's blend now ends at the slab's
   walls, a plain end;
3. what lies past the blends in the slabs: each blend face beside a slab's
   wall, its surface (a B-spline segmented to the face) prolonged across
   the slab at its end there by twice the slab's half width and past its
   contact away from the top by six (GeomLib, at C2), only its part within
   eight half widths of that end kept; a half slab round the blend's end
   section split by it and by the top's plane (BRepAlgoAPI_Splitter), and
   the piece holding the rim's corner taken;
4. each of those pieces less the fin's top there (the part under C's axis
   and inside C, prolonged along it, wider and longer than the slab, so
   that C and the fin's walls grow up to the blends), cut one by one; the
   body in the slabs less them all, fused with the rounded body (each of
   these booleans once for all the slabs, run in parallel); only a valid
   single solid is taken. (Cut all at once, the pieces of a slab's two
   halves, which touch, and the fins' tops sharing faces with them, came
   out wrong at some mouths.)
The result's history: each face from the body's face on its surface (or
the one it lies on), or generated from the rounded edge whose blend (or
prolonged blend) it lies on; the body's faces with no image removed
(Generated, Modified and IsDeleted read it). ChFi3d_Builder gives its
shape, parameters and StopChainsAtTurns setting (InitialShape, Params,
StopsChainsAtTurns, ChainTurnThrough). Every other fillet is as before.

On the stored design's body before the item: valid, 304 faces, the faces
of each body on the other's within 2 micrometres (OCCT's walked blend
against the file's along the arcs), the boolean differences of the two
empty both ways with a fuzzy value of 2 micrometres; the material within
a wedge of 6 degrees round a mouth 0.04 mm3 more than the file's, within
the arc beside it (39 degrees) 0.19 mm3 more; the rounding of the rim
takes about 10 s.

